# rm116, the rig-namer at the block (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm116
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent broad light
tier full
written_for rm_wynn_rustworth
written_by claude
title the rig-namer at the block
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=merchant temper=short
traits temper=short talk=chatty warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk calls_every_cut_on_the_block_after_a_rig
likes oatcakes cards sharp_knife misty_dawns
dislikes flies cold_tea
fears being_taken_for_a_beginner a_summer_of_flies
hopes a_block_with_my_own_name_over_it to_see_every_rig_on_the_sound_in_one_morning
topics ships +0.5 work +0.4 gossip +0.3 caravan +0.3 newcomer +0.3 prices +0.2 faith -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=eager refuse=short tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# talks fast and takes offence faster, is always first with whatever came through Northgate, keeps a knife that would split a hair, and calls every joint on the block after a kind of ship.

words
oath! = Sawdust and sails | Marrow and mast | Split me for a shank
saying! = You can't sell yesterday's news nor yesterday's mutton | Whatever the Sound brings in, somebody has to cut | First through the gate gets the best of it
block = the block | my block
cleaver = the cleaver | the heavy cleaver
knife = my boning knife | the thin knife
sawdust = clean sawdust | the sawdust
gate = the north gate | the gate
carts = the carts off the north road | the carts from up north
drovers = the drovers | the drove-folk off the hills
sound = the Sound | Ridgemere Sound
mist = the sea mist | a mist off the water
oatcake = buttered oatcake | an oatcake buttered edge to edge
flies = bluebottles | the flies
cards = cards | a hand of cards
coldtea = tea gone cold | stewed tea with no heat in it
counter = my counter | the meat counter
regulars = my regulars | the regulars
word = word | the word
rigs = rigs | sails
northtowns = Larkfield | Coldwater | Frosthollow
hall = the Market Hall | the Hall's long room
temper = my temper | the short end of me

any
opinion @1 city for 1 : Smoke, rain and all, it's mine. There's no town up the road gets news half as fast. | Everything worth knowing comes through #gate# or up off #sound#. Why would I live anywhere else?
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : I know what a carcass costs to walk down from the hills, so I'll not hear stallholders called thieves. | A fair price is a living, not a theft. Say otherwise at my counter and see how far you get.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New faces, new coin. I don't care where a wolf's from if the money's round. | Every newcomer's a regular I haven't met yet. I'd sooner sell to them than stare at them.
opinion @2 merchants for 1 [self.role!=merchant] : Stallholders earn their coin. Meat doesn't walk down off the hills for nothing.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : A traveller buys a pie and pays for it in news. That's a good bargain twice. | A stranger at #gate# has been somewhere I haven't. I'll have it out of them before they've found an inn.
opinion @1 road against 1 : #carts.capitalize# get slower by the season, and the whole city pays for every late hour. | The road north wants mending and watching, and it's getting neither.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : #oath#, they've had another wagon. Somebody up the hill wants to send #watch# out past the gate, not keep them warm in it.
opinion @1 watch for 1 [self.role=merchant] : A patrol past the Hall door and nobody's pinching from my hooks. I'll stand them a pie for that. | Grumble at #watch# all you want. I sleep, and my till's where I left it.
opinion @2 watch for 1 [self.role!=merchant] : Somebody walks the night streets so I needn't. Good enough for me.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : Grayrock's ships I'd watch all day. Grayrock's prices I'd not pay for a week. | The Houses are like weather. You don't argue with it, you work round it.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 1 : I like a feast day for the noise and hate it for the crush. | Half the city wants the same thing on the same morning. Lovely for some, ruin for the temper.
opinion @1 church mixed 0 : I go when I go. Prayers can find me at work as well as anywhere. | A candle for a ship coming home, yes. A sermon on a market morning, no.
opinion @1 times against 2 [world.times=hard|lean] : When folk start asking for bones and scrag ends, the times are bad, never mind what the Rain Hall says.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. You've caught me mid-thought. | #greet#, #address#. Talk away. Cutting's for paws, listening's for ears.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : New face. You came in by #gate#, I'll wager. | #greet#. New to me, that face. Not for long.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Step round the sawdust, I've only now spread it. | Stand clear of #block#, #address#. I'm mid-swing.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Early as a drover, you. | Morning. The mist's still on the water.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I've scrubbed down and I'm not getting mucky again. | Evening, #address#. I'm done for the day.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. I was half saying something anyway. | #greet#. Good. I'm bursting with something.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : There you are. Nobody's let me finish a sentence all morning. | You. Good. Sit, and don't touch my knife.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Talk fast, I've a side to break. | Go on. The cleaver's loud but I'm louder.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. If it's news, I want it. | Evening. The day's gossip is all but sold, but there's a scrap left.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : You'll never guess what came through the gate today. | Sit. I've kept this back since breakfast, just for you.
line @1 again.open : Twice in a morning. You're nearly a regular. | Back already. Either you forgot something or you heard something.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here. Come here. You'll want to hear this. | Oi. Over here, before somebody else tells you first.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. If you hear anything, I want it first. | #farewell#. Don't go telling it round before I have.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Mind the cobbles by the gate, they're slick. | Good night. I'll want the rest of that tomorrow.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. And keep your ears open. | Mind how you go. And bring me a story next time.
line @2 farewell.return [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : Away with you. There's a counter wants me. | Off you go. The regulars are queuing.
line @1 react.news : Wait. Say it again, slower. | Hang on. Who had it first?
line @1 react.news.new : That's not reached the gate. Where'd you get it? | #oath#. That got past me, and nothing gets past me.
line @1 react.news.known : Had that at dawn off the drovers. You're a morning behind. | Old news, #address#. It was all round Northgate before breakfast.
line @1 react.news.doubt : That's carried a long way and grown on the road. I'll wait. | Fetch me the one with eyes on it. Not a cousin of a cousin.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Oh, that's bad. That's a cruel business altogether. | #oath#. No. Poor souls, whoever they are.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Now that's worth hearing. I'll have it round the Hall by noon. | Good. Good! That's the first cheerful thing all week.
line @1 answer.how : Rushed. I'd sooner rushed than idle. | Well enough, if nobody tells me I'm holding the knife wrong.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : Run off my paws. There's a queue and three sides to break. | Busy. The regulars want everything at once, same as always.
line @1 ask.how : Road dust all over you. Been far? | You're wearing a look. Something happened.
line @1 sympathise : That's hard. Sit a while, the rest can wait. | I'm sorry. Truly. I don't say that to everybody.
line @1 reassure : Wait seven days. Most troubles shrink by then. | Don't fret yet. Fret when it's certain.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, no. I've work waiting and no paws spare today. | I can't, I'm sorry. My day's full to the brim, and it won't keep while I stand here.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair enough. I'll ask somebody less busy. | Right. I'll not ask twice.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. I'll file that beside the rigs, and I forget no rig. | Kindly done. I'll pay that back, see if I don't.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It's nothing. Anybody with paws would. | Never mind thanking me. Just tell me if you hear anything.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : I'd best get to the queue before it turns on me. | That's the bell. I've a side wants breaking.
line @2 preclose : Right. That's enough of my chatter for one morning. | Well. I've things to see to.
line @1 accept_preclose : Off with you. I've held you long enough. | Go, go. There's work waiting on me anyhow.
line @1 ack : Right. | Go on.
line @1 give.advice : Buy early, before the regulars clear the best of it. | Never trust a cut you can't see both sides of.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Stay inside the gate after dark. There's been trouble up the north road. | Don't go up the road alone this week, #address#.
line @1 take_offence : Nobody tells me my own trade. | Now just a moment. I heard that, and I didn't care for it.
line @1 accept_apology : Fine. Fine. I'm over it. Mostly. | Right. Say no more and we'll leave it.
line @1 insist : I'm telling you, the drovers told me themselves. | #insist#. Invent news? Me? I've never needed to.
line @1 congratulate : Well done, you. I'll be telling everyone. | That's grand. Good for you.
line @1 change_subject : Anyway. Did you see the sails out on the bay this morning? | Never mind that. Listen to this instead.
scene block_before_the_bell
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Sawdust down, block scrubbed, two sides hung, and the gate bell hasn't even gone yet. | Up before the drovers. I like the Hall empty, only the cleaver for company. | First knife in the Hall again. I check, you know. I look down the rows.
b : You must have been up in the dark. | Is it quiet, that early? | Does anybody else care who's first?
a : In the dark and the damp. Meat keeps no clock. | Quiet as the Sound on a still day. Then the regulars arrive, and it never is again till dusk. | They should. First knife gets first customers. That's sense, not vanity.

scene cuts_by_their_rigs
when topic=work|ships self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self / assess
a : That shoulder's a brig. Broad in the beam, sits heavy. I call every joint after a rig. | See this loin? That's a cutter. Long and lean and quick in the pan. | The little chops along the end are my luggers. Small, busy, and everybody wants one.
b : A brig? What makes it a brig? | And the leg, then? | What's the neck?
a : Two good masts of bone and a deep hold of meat. Same shape, if you squint. | The leg's a ketch. Stout, short in the mast, and it'll carry a family a week. | The neck's a fishing smack. Ugly, cheap, and better than it looks with a slow fire.
b : I'll not look at a joint the same way again. | You've thought about this a lot. | I'll ask for a smack next time.
a : Good. Nobody else in the Hall says it, and they should. | More than I'd admit to the regulars. | Call it a smack at my counter and I'll know you were listening.

scene flies_in_summer
when topic=work|weather here.season=summer self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Bluebottles. Every summer, every morning, as if they've a standing order. | Cloth on every joint, and in they come regardless. I could scream. | The heat's not the worst of summer. The flies are the worst of summer.
b : You can't keep them off meat. Nobody can. | It's the heat brings them up from the gutters. | Cooler days are coming.
a : I can, near enough, if I'm quick and I'm rude to them. | Then I'll keep the gutters as well, if that's what it takes. | Not soon enough. I'm counting the mornings.

scene came_late_to_the_knife
when topic=work|memories self.apprentice=yes self.role=merchant
does confide / react.self / reassure
a : I came to the block late, you know. Most of them in the Hall started as pups. | I'm still a learner at it, if I'm honest. Don't tell the regulars. | The one teaching me says I hold the cleaver as if I'd been born holding it. I wasn't.
b : You'd never know it. | Nobody would guess. | Then you've a gift for it.
a : I make sure you'd never know it. That's half the work. | Good. If they guessed, I'd hear about it every morning. | A gift, or I watched harder than the others. Either way.

scene the_hair_splitting_knife
when topic=pastimes|work self.likes=sharp_knife
does tell.boast / react.self / assess
a : Look at that edge. It'll split a hair down the long way. | I strop the thin knife each night, last thing, before the lamp goes out. | A blunt knife's an insult to the meat. Mine has never been blunt.
b : Mind your paws with it. | Every night? | Never?
a : I mind them. It's the blunt ones that slip. | Every night. I'd not rest otherwise. | Not once. Ask anybody in the Hall. Then ask them about theirs.

scene asking_for_bones
when topic=prices|times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / sympathise / assess
a : Bones and scrag, that's what they're asking for now. A bad week, then. | Same regulars, half the order. Nobody says why. Nobody has to. | I've started keeping the trimmings back for the ones who look thin.
b : It's the same everywhere this month. | That must be hard to watch. | That's decent of you.
a : I know. I hear it from every town that comes through the gate. | Harder to watch than to cut. I'll tell you that for nothing. | It's not decent. It's what you do. I'd want it done for me.

scene carts_late_north
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #carts.capitalize# are late again. I went to look at dawn and there was nothing but rain. | No drovers this morning. Not one. The gate's like a chapel on a market day. | Late. Again. I've a hook empty where a side ought to be hanging.
b : Is it the road, or the weather? | And with no stock, then? | How long can you wait?
a : Both, and somebody up the road too slow to mend either. | Sell what I've got slower and smile more. I'm bad at the smiling. | A day. Two, if I salt what's left. After that I'm selling sawdust.

scene carts_in_north
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=in self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : They're in! Carts, all of them! I knew it by the rumble on the cobbles before I saw a thing. | Drovers came through at dawn with a good string. I had the pick of it. | Everything's arrived from up north. The whole Hall's in a better mood.
b : That's a relief, after the wait. | You had the pick? | I could tell from the noise.
a : A relief and a half. I'd started dreaming about empty hooks. | First at the gate, first to choose. I've told you that before. | Noise means trade. I'd take a noisy Hall over a quiet one any day.

scene wagon_robbed_north
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided
does tell.news / react.news / assess
tone grave / serious / even
a : A wagon's been taken coming down. That's all anybody's saying, and they're saying it quiet. | A wagon robbed, coming down. The drovers came through white about the eyes. | Thieves out on the road once more. Meat and money gone, and maybe worse.
b : Was anyone hurt? | That's frightening. | Again? It's getting worse.
a : Nobody would say. When they won't say, I don't like it. | It is. And nobody in the Rain Hall walks that road, do they. | Worse every season. Mark me, the price of a chop will feel it before Restday.

scene the_hands_at_the_block
when topic=work|friends self.hand self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Till and thin knife both, I'd hand to #self.hand#. That's not nothing. | I've a good pair of paws at the block in #self.hand#. Not that I'd ever tell that face. | Without #self.hand# I'd be cutting and wrapping and talking all at once.
b : You should say it, sometimes. | Not nothing at all. | You'd manage.
a : I might. On a good day. | Not with me. I'm particular. | I'd manage worse. I'd manage loudly.

scene mist_on_the_sound
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Mist like this, and the masts swim up through it one after another. Nothing in the city beats it. | I stood on the top step this morning and couldn't see the Sound at all. Only the bells. | You can tell a rig by its outline in mist, if you've the eyes. I've the eyes.
b : It's only fog. | Only the bells? | Can you really?
a : Only fog! Only fog, says you. There's no poetry in you at all. | Bells, and a gull or two. Then a topsail, all at once, like a ghost coming in. | Brig, schooner, ketch. Dim or not, I'm never wrong.

scene misty_dawn_walk
when topic=weather|day here.time=dawn self.likes=misty_dawns
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I came down while the steps were still grey with mist. I'd get up early for that alone. | There's no hour like a misty dawn. Nobody's shouting yet. Even me. | A grey dawn and wet whiskers. You can keep your summer.
b : Most folk would rather stay in bed. | Even you? | Wet whiskers?
a : Then most folk sleep through the finest hour. | Even me, for an hour. After that, all bets are off. | Wet whiskers and a clean head. That's the trade.

scene a_ship_in
when topic=ships topic.kind=in|arrived topic.ship here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s in. I had the masts in sight from the high steps before anyone at the Hall. | Did you see? #topic.ship.capitalize#, made fast at the piers on the dawn tide. | #topic.ship.capitalize# came in while I was hanging the first side. I heard the shout from the piers.
b : How did you know it from that far? | Is it the one you were waiting on? | You heard it from the Hall?
a : By the rake of the masts. Every rig leans its own way, if you know it. | I wait on every ship. That's how I'm made. | From the Hall door. The wind was right. I'd know that shout anywhere.

scene a_ship_due
when topic=ships topic.kind=due topic.ship
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s due. I'll be looking down the Sound between customers. | #topic.ship.capitalize#'s expected, they say. I'll know it before it clears the headland. | #topic.ship.capitalize#, expected any hour. My neck's sore from watching the bay since breakfast.
b : You'll spot it first, I expect. | Before it clears the headland? | Rest your neck a while.
a : I generally do. It's no trick, just looking. | By the cut of the sails. You'd see it too if you bothered. | Not till it's in. I'll be first to say so, and that's what counts.

scene a_ship_late
when topic=ships topic.kind=late topic.ship
does tell.worry / reassure / insist / ack
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s late. Two tides. I don't like it. | Still no #topic.ship#. I've stopped looking, and I'm still looking. | #topic.ship.capitalize#, late. One fool at the Hall muttered the word lost. I near threw a chop.
b : Ships run late all the time. | You've stopped looking? | They spoke out of turn.
a : Ships do run late. I've said it myself a hundred times. I'll still not sleep. | I'll look again at noon. I'll pretend it's for the weather. | They did. Not twice, though. Not near my block.
b? : #bc_maybe# | #bc_i_know# | #bc_true#

scene a_ship_lost
when topic=ships topic.kind=lost topic.ship
does tell.news / sympathise / assess
tone grave / tender / even
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s lost. I knew that rig coming in a mile off. | They say #topic.ship# went down. I've no chatter in me this morning. | #topic.ship.capitalize#. Gone. #oath#. The whole Hall's gone quiet with it.
b : I'm sorry. I know you watched for it. | That's a heavy thing. | Rest easy, all aboard.
a : Every time it came in. I'll miss the sight of it, and worse for the ones who'll miss more. | It is. Somebody's waiting at home for that crew. That's the part I can't cut round. | Bright One keep them, every one aboard.

scene a_ship_sailed
when topic=ships topic.kind=sailed|left topic.ship
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s gone out. I watched it to the headland between two customers. | There goes #topic.ship#, riding the ebb out. Clean wake, every sail full. | #topic.ship.capitalize# sailed at first light. Sitting deep, so somebody filled the hold.
b : You never miss one. | Every sail full? | Somebody did well, then.
a : I miss them all. That's why I watch. | Every one. Pretty as anything in the Rain Hall. | Somebody always does. It's never me, but somebody.

scene tell_a_rig
when topic=ships|pastimes here.region=ridgemere here.outdoors=clear|overcast
does challenge / accept / assess / ack
a : Look out on the Sound. That one with two masts and the big square sails. That's a brig. | See the little one with the one mast and the long boom? That's a cutter, and quick with it. | That fat one, tall mast forward, short one aft. That's a ketch. Now you say it.
b : A brig. Two masts, square sails. | A cutter. One mast, long boom. | A ketch. Tall forward, short aft.
a : There. You know more than half the Hall now. | Good. Next time I'll test you, and I'll not be kind. | Right. You'll be calling my joints by them next.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_maybe#

scene pass_it_on
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=other|prowling|wanted|promise_broken
does tell.gossip / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Here's one. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. Mine first, mind. | Listen. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. It was round the gate before noon. | You never heard this from me. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#.
b : Who told you that? | True, would you say? | Why the secrecy?
a : A carter off the north road, who swore to a cousin who saw. Close enough. | True enough to repeat. I'll not swear to more. | No secrecy. I only want to tell it again myself before you do.

scene gossip_cruel
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=death|violence|theft|victim
does tell.gossip / sympathise / assess
tone serious / grave / even
a : You'll have heard. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. I wish I hadn't. | It's all round the Hall. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. Nobody's laughing today. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. That's the word at the gate, and it's a hard one.
b : That's a terrible thing. | I'd not heard. I'm sorry. | Bright One keep #topic.them#.
a : It is. Anyone who laughs at it can buy their meat elsewhere. | You'll hear it again by dusk, and worse told. I'd sooner you had it straight. | Bright One keep #topic.them#, and keep us out of it.

scene gossip_sweet
when topic=gossip|newcomer here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=marriage|newcomer|promise_kept
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : Now here's one. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. I've been grinning since breakfast. | Good news, for once. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. I had it first. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. The Hall's talked of nothing else.
b : That's lovely to hear. | Good for #topic.them#. | You'd have heard it first, wouldn't you.
a : Isn't it? I'll have it round the whole of Northgate by dark. | Good for #topic.them#, and for my till too. Somebody will order a roast. | Course I did. It's my gate.

scene what_the_carts_said
when topic=news world.news
does tell.news / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Word off the road from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. I had it before the toll was paid. | The drovers brought it down from #world.news_from#. #world.news.capitalize#. | You'll want this. From #world.news_from#: #world.news#.
b : Before the toll was paid? | Do you believe it? | Who else knows?
a : Before the drover's paws were dry. I ask quick. | I believe the drovers. They've no reason to lie, only to exaggerate. | By now, half the Hall. By dark, everyone. I can't help it.

scene new_face_at_the_gate
when topic=newcomer|player here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Somebody new in Northgate. Came in by the gate, I'd say, by the mud on them. | Somebody new's been wandering Northgate like a lost hen. They'll know us all inside seven days. | Another stranger off the road. That's coin walking about with nowhere to go yet.
b : You notice everyone. | You'll win them over. | Coin with nowhere to go?
a : Everyone who comes through the gate. Which is everyone. | I'll win them or I'll know why. | Not for long. Not with me on the corner.

scene hard_times_north
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does give.opinion / agree / assess
a : The drovers say it's worse up the road than here, and here's bad enough. | What folk buy tells you the times. It's the cheap end, now. | Hard all over. Worse up north, to hear the carters.
b : It's bad everywhere. | Times always come round. | And the Rain Hall does nothing.
a : Everywhere, and the price of a side still goes up. Explain that to me. | They'd better come round soon. I can't sell bones forever. | Nothing I can see. Somebody up the hill should come and stand at a counter.

scene good_times_north
when topic=times world.times=good|boom self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / agree / assess
a : The good joints are selling again. Folk walk taller with a roast to carry. | Every cart through the gate is loaded, and every regular wants a roast. | I've run out of loin twice this week. I'll not complain about that.
b : Good while it lasts. | Busy's better than quiet. | Twice? That's a good week.
a : It'll last while I'm quick. I'm always quick. | It is. And it's better still when the till's heavy. | The best in a long while. I'll sleep well tonight.

scene feast_morning_rush
when topic=festival|season world.festival=today|soon self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Feast coming. Half the city wants a roast the same morning. | By dawn the queue will be out into the street. I've told them to order ahead. They never do. | Feast days fill the till and empty my patience.
b : You'll do well out of it. | They never do? | Empty your patience?
a : I'll do well and I'll be furious doing it. That's every feast. | Never. Then they're cross with me when the joints are gone. | Mine's short already. By noon, it's gone altogether.

scene buttered_oatcake
when topic=food self.likes=oatcakes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Hand me #oatcake# and all's forgiven. Nearly all. | I keep two oatcakes wrapped by the block for when the queue goes. | A buttered oatcake at noon. That's the day halfway won.
b : Nearly? | Only two? | Halfway?
a : Nearly. There's a limit, and folk find it. | Two. If I kept three I'd eat three. | Halfway. Keeping my temper till dusk wins the rest.

scene tea_gone_cold
when topic=food|smalltalk self.dislikes=cold_tea
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Made a cup first thing, found it at noon, stone cold. I could cry. | Cold tea. There's nothing sadder in a cup. | Somebody handed me stewed tea with no heat in it, and smiled. Smiled.
b : You could warm it again. | Drink it quicker. | They meant well.
a : Warmed-over tea is worse. It knows what it's done. | Drink it quicker. As if I've a spare moment to drink anything. I'll try that. | Meaning well is how folk get away with it.

scene cards_at_night
when topic=pastimes self.likes=cards here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / tease / tease.return / ack
a : A hand of cards after the scrub-down. I play to win, mind. | Three hands lost, one after another, last night. I've not slept for it. | Sit down across from me and I'll deal. Expect no mercy.
b : You don't like losing. | Three in a row? Bad luck. | I might hold my own.
a : Nobody likes losing. I just say so louder. | It's not luck. Somebody was cheating, and I'll prove it. | You might. Or you'll be buying the oatcakes. One or the other.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_bad_luck# | #bc_we_will_see#

scene the_office_drawers
when topic=smalltalk|day here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The City Office loses paper the way the Sound loses hats. Nobody knows where it goes. | I'd sooner wait on a late cart than on a clerk. A cart at least comes in the end. | Ask the City Office a thing and they point you to another window.
b : Waiting on them for what? | Have you complained? | Which window?
a : Something filed and never seen again. I go back and ask. They gaze at me as if I were the weather. | To who? There's a form for complaining, and it's lost as well. | The next one. Always the next one along. I've stood at every one.

scene rain_at_the_gate
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.region=ridgemere self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : It's coming down so hard the drovers came through the gate looking drowned. | Rain on the Hall roof like gravel. I can hardly hear the customers. Fine by me, some days. | Wet sawdust, wet paws, and every regular dripping on my counter.
b : It always rains on the gate. | Fine by you? | You can't stop the rain.
a : The gate's a funnel. Everything off the hills comes down it, rain and all. | Some of them say things I'm better off not hearing. | No, but grumbling's free, and I'm generous with it.

scene housemate_trust
when topic=home|friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend# and I can quarrel at breakfast and forget it by supper. | I'd trust #self.friend# with my knives. Nobody else gets that. | When the Hall's shut, #self.friend#'s the one I tell things to.
b : That's worth a lot. | From you, that's a great deal. | Does #self.friend# listen?
a : It is. Just never within earshot. | It is. That stays between us. | Most of the time. Enough to keep me from bursting.

scene a_cut_paw
when topic=health self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : I nicked a pad on the bone saw. My own fault, I was talking. | My paws ache by dusk. It's the cold of the meat, not the work. | Somebody told me I look tired. I near took offence.
b : You talk a lot while you work. | Warm them by the fire. | You do look a bit tired.
a : Then I'll talk less. I'll fail, but I'll mean it. | I do, and they mend by morning. That's enough. | Well. Fine. Maybe a little. Don't spread it about.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_good# | #bc_sorry#

scene teased_and_bristling
when topic=smalltalk|work pair.band=friends|acquaintances self.role=merchant
does tell.self / tease / take_offence / apologise / accept_apology
a : I can name every joint on the block after a rig. Nobody else in the Hall can. | The drovers gave me that before the gate was even open. | I tied that parcel myself. Under, round and pull.
b : Nobody else in the Hall wants to, I'd guess. | You'd have it off the gulls if they could talk. | It looks like it was tied in the dark.
a : Now that's uncalled for. Civil, if you please. | And what's that supposed to mean? I listen, that's all. | I'll have you know that knot's held a side of beef.
b : I didn't mean anything by it. | Only that you hear everything. It's a compliment. | All right, all right. It's a good knot.
a : Fine. I'm over it. I'm always over it by noon. | Then say it like one next time. Right. We'll leave it. | It's a very good knot. Say no more and we'll leave it there.

scene the_bright_one_and_ships
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : A candle for every late ship. The Bright One must know my voice by now. | Sermons go over my head. A word at the water's edge does me. | The chapel by the water's the only one I'll go into without being dragged.
b : Better that than nothing. | That's enough for most. | Why that one?
a : True. I could be praying for my own temper, and I'd be there all day. | It must. The day won't open itself. | It faces the Sound. I can pray and look at the rigs at the same time.

scene the_sound_and_the_city
when topic=lore|land here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Every town up the north road thinks itself grand. Then the carters see the Sound, and go quiet. | The city's built on the Sound's back, and the Sound doesn't care. | High in Northgate there's a step where the whole bay shows, and half the realm's business sailing in.
b : They do go quiet. | Doesn't care? | Half the realm's business?
a : Without fail. I watch for it. It's my favourite thing at the gate. | Not a bit. It was here first. It'll be here after the Rain Hall falls in. | Near enough. The other half comes through the gate. I watch both.

scene towns_up_the_road
when topic=travel|caravan here.region=ridgemere self.away=no
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The carters from #northtowns# talk slower than us. You can sell them anything while they're still saying hello. | Past the gate? Never been. Why would I go? The towns come to me. | Ask a drover what's dear up north and you'll know what'll be dear here in a week.
b : Never past the gate? | That's a sly trick. | That's useful.
a : There's no need. All I'd want to see walks in on its own. | Not sly. Patient. I'm not patient, but I manage it with carters. | It is. And they'll tell you, if you've a pie in your paw.

story first_side_alone [topic=work|memories self.apprentice=yes self.role=merchant]
  : The first side I broke down alone, the one teaching me stood at my back, silent the whole while.
  : I could hear every breath. I got through the shoulder, the loin, the leg, and only then turned round.
  ask : What do you suppose was said, after all that?
  b : Something kind, I hope.
  : Not a thing. A nod, and a fresh side hung up beside it. I've never been prouder of a nod.

story three_masters [topic=ships|memories self.age>=18 here.region=ridgemere]
  : As a pup I'd sit up on the north wall counting masts till my eyes ran.
  : One morning three tall ships came in together out of the mist, one behind the other, like a procession.
  ask : Can you picture a thing like that, coming out of nothing?
  b : I can try. It must have been a sight.
  : I named every rig that day and nobody believed me. I've not stopped since.

story the_losing_hand [topic=pastimes self.likes=cards]
  : I once lost a whole week's oatcakes to a carter at cards, one hand after another.
  : I kept saying the next one's mine, and the next one never was.
  ask : Would you have stopped, or kept going?
  b : I'd have stopped after the second, I think.
  : Sense, that. I kept going. I've never sat down with a carter since without counting the deck first.

story topsail_in_the_mist [topic=weather|ships self.likes=misty_dawns here.region=ridgemere]
  : I came down the steps one grey dawn and a topsail slid out of the mist so close I could have hailed it.
  : Nobody else on the steps. Only me, and that great grey sail, and the water slapping.
  b : That must have given you a start. Did you hail it?
  : Course I did. Somebody aboard waved back. The Hall opened without me that morning and I didn't care a bit.

story the_old_drover [topic=travel|caravan|memories self.age>=18]
  : As a pup I knew an old drover who'd stop at our door and name the towns for me, one by one, as far as the snow.
  : He'd say what each one smelled of. Wool, smoke, snow, cheese. I learned them like a song.
  ask : What would you say this city smells of, if you had to?
  b : Rain and smoke, I'd say. And the sea.
  : That's it. And meat, round my end. I never did get to tell him that.

rule @block_cry
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Fresh off the block! Brigs and cutters, come and see! | Shoulders, loins, chops and shanks! Cut while you wait!

rule @bluebottles
when concept=bark self.working=yes here.season=summer
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Off. Off! Go and bother the fishmongers. | Another one. I'll have every fly in Northgate by noon.

rule @mast_spotted
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 10h
say That's a brig coming round the headland. Told you. | Two masts, square rigged, low in the water. In by noon.

rule @carts_wanted
when concept=bark world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Still no carts. Still no carts! | Where are those drovers? I've empty hooks.

rule @wet_sawdust
when concept=bark here.weather=rain self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Wet paws on my counter again. | Rain through the Hall door, and the sawdust soaking.

rule @cold_cup
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Cold. It's gone cold again. | Who left my cup to die?

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. If you've news, I'm listening. If not, I'm cutting. | #greet#, #address#. Quick, before the queue comes back.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Anything new through the gate that I've missed? | #topic.player#. There you are. Talk, I'm all ears.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Evening. I've done my day, so whatever it is, it's talk only. | It's late, #address#. What's wanted?
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Out with it, plainly. I've no time for faces. | You've a look I don't care for. Well?
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant self.working=yes] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I break the sides, cut the joints and call them after ships, and the regulars buy them anyway. | Meat. Every cut on the block, and a word for every one.
dlg @2 resident.job : Busy hands, open ears. In this city that's most of a living. | Whatever needs doing, and quickly. I'm not good at slow.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Bring me something worth hearing next time. | #farewell#, #address#. Don't be a stranger.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Good night. Mind the cobbles at the gate. | Good night. I'll want the rest tomorrow.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Off the carts from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. Had it before anyone, mind. | Word from #world.news_from#, in at dawn with the drovers: #world.news#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : It's all #world.event_words# at the Hall. You'll hear nothing else today.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers took a wagon on its way down to us. The drovers came in pale. Have a care on the road.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : A burying on our street this week. Northgate's been hushed since, and so have I, near enough.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : A wedding! I sold the roast for it. Best order of the month.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# And that's not for want of asking, believe me. | Nothing through the gate worth repeating. You'd have had it from me first if there were.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's how it reached me. Plain, as I had it.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=death|violence] : It's a sad one. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. I'll not say more.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# Which is a first, for me. | Nothing today. The gate's been dull as ditchwater.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Every hook bare by noon, and loin gone twice this week. | Busy enough to want two more paws.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Folk eat, and I cut. The rent's covered and then some. | Steady enough. I'd do better if the carts kept time.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly. Folk are buying the cheap end, and not much of that. | Bad, and no use hiding it. The hooks are full and the till isn't.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop=open] : #greet#. Brigs, cutters and luggers today. Shoulders, loins and chops, if you're not a sailor. | #greet#, #address#. Name the joint and I'll cut it to the weight.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop=open topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. I put a good cutter by, hoping you'd come. | #greet#, #topic.player#. The usual cut, or a ketch for a change?
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. Bare hooks, I'm afraid, till the drovers are in. I'd buy meat off you, mind. | Nothing hanging. The carts have let me down again.
dlg @1 shop.prices : #shop_dear# Mutton goes with the carts. When they're late, it's dear. | It's all on the slate. Ask me tomorrow and the slate will have changed.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #shop_bye# Cook it slow, mind. | #farewell#. Tell them where you bought it.
graft resident.hub
choice Can you really tell every ship on the Sound? [here.region=ridgemere] -> rigs
node rigs
say Every rig, yes. Not every name; I'm no harbourmaster. A brig's two masts and square sails. A cutter's one mast and a long boom. That's the start.
mood keen
free
choice Why do you care so much? -> rigs_why
choice Back to what I was asking. -> hub
node rigs_why
say Everything this city has comes in on one, or through the gate. And they're lovely, and nobody in the Hall will admit it but me.
mood pleased
choice I'll look at them differently now. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Why do you call that joint a brig? [here.shop=open] -> brig
node brig
say Look at it. Broad in the beam, two masts of bone, a deep hold of meat. A brig. The loin's a cutter, the chops are luggers, the neck's a fishing smack.
mood keen
free
choice I'll take a brig, then. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
